{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Finding\n\nFound while reviewing the ADR 0117 draft (task `arterial-bilineate-vampirebat` /\n`a01e-01e7-6e6e-4809`) against the tree. The draft cited `src/rebar/reducer/marker.py:33` as one of\nthree sibling modules that \"already guard correctly\", offered as evidence that `lock.py` is an\noutlier. Re-reading the file shows the opposite: **`marker.py` has the identical defect.**\n\n```\nsrc/rebar/reducer/marker.py:9-11    try: import fcntl / except ImportError: fcntl = None\nsrc/rebar/reducer/marker.py:33      fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)      # write_marker,  unguarded\nsrc/rebar/reducer/marker.py:38      fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)      # write_marker,  unguarded\nsrc/rebar/reducer/marker.py:61      fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX)      # remove_marker,  unguarded\nsrc/rebar/reducer/marker.py:67      fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)      # remove_marker,  unguarded\n```\n\nThe import is guarded to `None`; the four uses are not. On Windows every one raises\n`AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'flock'`.\n\nThe two genuine counter-examples in the draft do hold — `_snapshot/git_fetch.py:79` and\n`_snapshot/janitor.py:430` both branch on `if fcntl is not None` before use. `marker.py` was the\nthird citation and it is wrong.\n\n## Why the surrounding `try/except OSError` does not contain it\n\nBoth functions wrap the locked region in `try: ... except OSError:` and log a warning, documented as\n\"On any OSError: logs a warning to stderr and returns without raising. Failed marker writes must not\nprevent callers from proceeding.\" **`AttributeError` is not an `OSError`**, so it is not caught: it\npropagates out of a function whose contract says it never raises. The `finally:` at `:37`/`:66` then\nruns `fcntl.flock(lock_fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)`, which raises a *second* `AttributeError` from the\ncleanup path and replaces the first.\n\nSo this is not merely \"another Windows failure\". It breaks a documented never-raises contract, and\nit does so through the `finally` block, which is the shape that loses the original error.\n\n## Implementation approach\n\nDefault now: implement the local `if fcntl is not None` guard in `marker.py`.\n`origin/main` does not yet expose a blocking shared helper for this module, and reusing a\nnon-blocking selector would change the existing POSIX blocking semantics. When the shared\nblocking helper is available, a follow-up can consolidate this file onto it. The no-fcntl\npath remains best-effort: marker writes are single-path marker create/remove operations,\n`write_marker` uses an atomic temp-write-and-rename, and `remove_marker` is an idempotent\nunlink; without a platform lock the functions must not raise and must not corrupt partial\nmarker contents.\n\n## Relationship to S1\n\nSeparate from `S1` (`friendless-alabaster-cub`), which owns `_store/lock.py` and\n`_store/lock_owner.py` and is actively in flight. This ticket must NOT touch those files. It is\nlisted under the same epic because it is the same root cause in a different module, and because\n`S1`'s Windows exclusive-lock leg may supply the mechanism this file should call rather than\nopen-coding a second one.\n\nSequence after `S1`: this ticket now uses the local guard because the shared blocking\nhelper is not available on this branch; a later consolidation can replace it with the shared\nhelper without changing the tested behaviour.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `write_marker` and `remove_marker` do not raise `AttributeError` when `fcntl is None`; a test\n   proves it by patching `rebar.reducer.marker.fcntl` to `None`.\n- [ ] The documented never-raises contract holds under `fcntl is None` — the functions return normally,\n   and the `finally` unlock path does not raise.\n- [ ] Serialization is still enforced on POSIX (the existing behaviour is unchanged when `fcntl` is\n   available), proven by a test that still exercises the real lock.\n- [ ] Neither `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` nor `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` is modified.\n\n\n\n## Testing\n\n- RED: `pytest tests/unit/test_marker_fcntl_guard.py -q` fails on unmodified main by patching `rebar.reducer.marker.fcntl` to `None`.\n- GREEN: `pytest tests/unit/test_marker_fcntl_guard.py -q` passes after the guard.\n- POSIX regression: `pytest tests/unit/test_marker_fcntl_guard.py tests/unit -k marker -q` exercises the real fcntl path where available.\n- Final gates: `make verify`, plus the known `GIT_CONFIG` isolation artifact is checked solo with the environment variables unset."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788867732176425001,"uuid":"a5b84db4-2bf2-468b-8b1d-3a0e04e9a0d2"}